<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Fontainebleau (1762)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1762">1762</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Fontainebleau (1762) was a secret agreement of 3 November 1762 in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, to its ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Made as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> was ending, it compensated Spain for losing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and relieved France of a colony it could no longer defend. It created Spanish Louisiana, which Spain returned to France in 1800.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty followed the Battle of Signal Hill in September 1762, the last battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-and-indian-war">French and Indian War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, which confirmed British control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> went on. Having lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> proposed to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a> that France give <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> the country known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> and the island on which the city stands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Charles ratified the agreement on 13 November 1762 and Louis ten days later. It was kept secret even while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> negotiated and signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in February 1763, which ended the war. That treaty divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, giving the eastern half to Britain and leaving the western half and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> nominally with France. Spain, which knew it would rule western Louisiana and had ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to Britain under the same peace, did not contest Britain's hold on the east. The Treaty of Paris also allowed French colonists 18 months to leave British territory, and many moved to Louisiana without knowing it had already been given to Spain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The cession was finally made public in 1764. In a letter of 21 April 1764 Louis informed the governor, Jean-Jacques Blaise d'Abbadie, expressing the hope that the Spanish king would give his new subjects the protection and goodwill that the misfortunes of war had kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from offering. The colonists of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> refused the change and expelled the first Spanish governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-ulloa">Antonio de Ulloa</a>, in the Louisiana Rebellion of 1768. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alejandro-oreilly">Alejandro O'Reilly</a>, an Irish émigré, put down the rebellion and took formal possession for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1769. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to Spain in 1783, Spanish territory encircled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Fontainebleau (1762)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the secret <b>Treaty of Fontainebleau</b> of 1762 France handed French Louisiana west of the Mississippi, together with New Orleans, to its ally Spain as compensation for losses in the Seven Years' War.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-louisiana">Spanish Louisiana</a></li></ul></section>
